1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,370
This is Allowance: agentic wallet infrastructure on Solana.

2
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:07,264
It gives every AI agent its own wallet key,

3
00:00:07,314 --> 00:00:10,696
with spending rules the blockchain itself enforces.

4
00:00:11,996 --> 00:00:16,173
AI agents are starting to pay for things: APIs, data, other people.

5
00:00:16,223 --> 00:00:18,381
But today you have two bad options.

6
00:00:18,431 --> 00:00:20,400
Hand the agent your private key,

7
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:23,807
and one prompt injection or runaway loop can drain it.

8
00:00:23,857 --> 00:00:27,845
Or guard it with a server, which can be bypassed or compromised.

9
00:00:27,895 --> 00:00:30,810
Allowance is a wallet built for agents instead.

10
00:00:32,110 --> 00:00:36,329
Here is the site. The headline says it plainly: wallets for AI agents,

11
00:00:36,379 --> 00:00:38,159
with rules the chain enforces.

12
00:00:38,209 --> 00:00:41,818
Every payment an agent makes is checked by a Solana program,

13
00:00:41,868 --> 00:00:43,892
not by a server you have to trust.

14
00:00:45,192 --> 00:00:49,342
The infrastructure has four layers. A vault, owned by the program,

15
00:00:49,392 --> 00:00:51,378
that only you can withdraw from.

16
00:00:51,428 --> 00:00:55,832
A session key for each agent, with its own caps, merchants and expiry.

17
00:00:55,882 --> 00:00:59,650
An MCP server, which is how Claude or Cursor use the wallet.

18
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,767
And the policy engine, the agent_pay instruction,

19
00:01:02,817 --> 00:01:05,758
which checks every rule inside the transaction.

20
00:01:07,058 --> 00:01:10,914
The landing page walks through the same story, with live motion:

21
00:01:10,964 --> 00:01:14,209
the four layers, the 7 wallet tools an agent gets,

22
00:01:14,259 --> 00:01:17,321
how a payment is checked, the real devnet demo run,

23
00:01:17,371 --> 00:01:19,152
and what a refusal looks like.

24
00:01:20,452 --> 00:01:22,054
Here is the full workflow.

25
00:01:22,104 --> 00:01:24,976
You ask Claude to pay your friend 1 USDC.

26
00:01:25,026 --> 00:01:28,723
Claude calls the transfer tool on the Allowance MCP server,

27
00:01:28,773 --> 00:01:29,613
with a reason.

28
00:01:29,663 --> 00:01:32,408
The server resolves friend from the session,

29
00:01:32,458 --> 00:01:35,965
and signs an agent_pay transaction with the agent's key.

30
00:01:36,015 --> 00:01:37,553
The program then decides.

31
00:01:37,603 --> 00:01:40,856
A yes moves USDC from the vault to the recipient.

32
00:01:40,906 --> 00:01:43,778
A no fails on-chain, with the rule that fired.

33
00:01:45,078 --> 00:01:48,292
The Solana program is written in Rust with Anchor.

34
00:01:48,342 --> 00:01:50,054
It has 12 instructions.

35
00:01:50,104 --> 00:01:54,623
Ten are for the owner, signed with Phantom: create the vault, deposit,

36
00:01:54,673 --> 00:01:57,560
withdraw, freeze, create and revoke sessions,

37
00:01:57,610 --> 00:01:59,583
and approve or reject requests.

38
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:04,022
Only two can be signed by an agent: agent_pay, and request approval.

39
00:02:04,072 --> 00:02:08,395
So a leaked agent key can never spend more than its session allows.

40
00:02:08,445 --> 00:02:12,964
Agent pay runs 12 checks in order, and each one has its own error.

41
00:02:14,264 --> 00:02:16,130
Here's what it's built with.

42
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:18,936
The program uses Anchor and SPL tokens,

43
00:02:18,986 --> 00:02:24,274
and reads merchant reputation from the 8004 Agent Registry.

44
00:02:24,324 --> 00:02:28,654
The agent side is a Model Context Protocol server in TypeScript.

45
00:02:28,704 --> 00:02:31,323
Payments to APIs use the x402 standard,

46
00:02:31,373 --> 00:02:34,813
with a facilitator and a demo paid API in the repo.

47
00:02:34,863 --> 00:02:38,440
The dashboard is Next.js 16 with React 19,

48
00:02:38,490 --> 00:02:41,520
TanStack Query and the Solana wallet adapter.

49
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:44,257
A shared client package holds the IDL,

50
00:02:44,307 --> 00:02:46,926
instruction builders and PDA helpers.

51
00:02:48,226 --> 00:02:52,740
Now the app itself. Connect Phantom, or view the demo vault read-only,

52
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:53,653
as we do here.

53
00:02:53,703 --> 00:02:57,304
The overview shows your agents' wallet: active sessions,

54
00:02:57,354 --> 00:03:01,281
today's spending, recent transactions, and the vault balance.

55
00:03:01,331 --> 00:03:04,736
You fund it from Phantom, or by scanning the QR code.

56
00:03:06,036 --> 00:03:10,311
The dashboard has 7 pages, one for each part of the wallet:

57
00:03:10,361 --> 00:03:13,880
overview, vault, agent sessions, contacts, activity,

58
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:15,528
approvals and merchants.

59
00:03:15,578 --> 00:03:19,990
A sliding indicator follows you between tabs, each page eases in,

60
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:23,765
and everything you see is read straight from the chain,

61
00:03:23,815 --> 00:03:25,138
not from a database.

62
00:03:26,438 --> 00:03:30,713
The vault page is where you withdraw, which works even while frozen,

63
00:03:30,763 --> 00:03:32,749
and where the kill switch lives.

64
00:03:32,799 --> 00:03:36,756
Freeze stops every payment from every agent, on-chain, at once.

65
00:03:38,056 --> 00:03:41,104
To give an agent access, you issue it a session.

66
00:03:41,154 --> 00:03:43,493
The MCP server prints its public key.

67
00:03:43,543 --> 00:03:47,366
Paste it in, name the session, and choose how long it lasts.

68
00:03:48,666 --> 00:03:50,247
Then set the limits: here,

69
00:03:50,297 --> 00:03:53,571
at most 0.05 USDC per payment and 2 USDC a day,

70
00:03:53,621 --> 00:03:55,767
with listed merchants and contacts,

71
00:03:55,817 --> 00:03:58,903
and a reputation floor of 70 for anyone else.

72
00:03:58,953 --> 00:04:02,479
The panel on the right restates the rules in plain words.

73
00:04:02,529 --> 00:04:05,176
Sign with Phantom, and the session is live.

74
00:04:06,476 --> 00:04:11,266
Agent sessions lists every agent key with its status, today's spending,

75
00:04:11,316 --> 00:04:12,834
its cap and its expiry.

76
00:04:14,134 --> 00:04:17,815
Open one to see its meters against each cap: the daily budget,

77
00:04:17,865 --> 00:04:21,847
each merchant's own cap, and the shared cap for unlisted merchants.

78
00:04:21,897 --> 00:04:26,060
You can revoke any single agent from here without touching the others.

79
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:32,221
Connecting an agent takes one config block, in Claude Desktop or Cursor.

80
00:04:32,271 --> 00:04:36,791
Point it at the MCP server, your Phantom address and the agent key.

81
00:04:36,841 --> 00:04:41,360
The agent then gets 7 tools: get allowance, transfer, pay x402,

82
00:04:41,410 --> 00:04:45,862
request approval, check approval, check merchant, and get history.

83
00:04:47,162 --> 00:04:48,676
And then you just talk to it.

84
00:04:48,726 --> 00:04:52,075
Ask what your allowance is, and Claude reads it from the chain.

85
00:04:52,125 --> 00:04:55,150
Ask for something over the cap, and the chain refuses it.

86
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:57,092
Claude tells you exactly which rule,

87
00:04:57,142 --> 00:04:59,088
and can ask you for approval instead.

88
00:05:00,388 --> 00:05:02,151
Paid APIs work over x402.

89
00:05:02,201 --> 00:05:06,504
The API answers 402, payment required, with a price.

90
00:05:06,554 --> 00:05:10,494
The MCP server builds agent_pay for exactly that price.

91
00:05:10,544 --> 00:05:13,975
A facilitator verifies every rule by simulation,

92
00:05:14,025 --> 00:05:16,950
co-signs as the fee payer and submits it.

93
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,214
USDC moves from the vault to the merchant,

94
00:05:20,264 --> 00:05:22,535
and the API returns the content.

95
00:05:22,585 --> 00:05:26,162
If a rule fails, the refusal still lands on-chain.

96
00:05:27,462 --> 00:05:30,916
Every result shows up in Activity: paid or refused,

97
00:05:30,966 --> 00:05:35,932
with the agent's reason and a link to the transaction on Solana Explorer.

98
00:05:37,232 --> 00:05:40,744
Refusals are highlighted in gold with the rule that fired:

99
00:05:40,794 --> 00:05:45,105
over the per-request cap, low reputation, a missing reputation account,

100
00:05:45,155 --> 00:05:47,070
or a payment tried while frozen.

101
00:05:48,370 --> 00:05:51,682
When an agent needs more than its cap allows, it asks.

102
00:05:51,732 --> 00:05:54,173
The request waits on the Approvals page,

103
00:05:54,223 --> 00:05:57,659
and you approve or reject it with one Phantom signature.

104
00:05:57,709 --> 00:06:01,208
An approval pays once, for that merchant and that amount.

105
00:06:02,508 --> 00:06:04,837
Merchants shows who your agents may pay,

106
00:06:04,887 --> 00:06:07,156
with today's spending against each cap,

107
00:06:07,206 --> 00:06:10,426
and each merchant's reputation from the Agent Registry.

108
00:06:11,726 --> 00:06:14,016
Contacts lets agents pay people by name.

109
00:06:14,066 --> 00:06:17,700
You can test how a name resolves before the agent ever uses it,

110
00:06:17,750 --> 00:06:19,572
and it never guesses an address.

111
00:06:20,872 --> 00:06:24,637
The How it works page documents the system: the pieces,

112
00:06:24,687 --> 00:06:28,245
every rule agent_pay checks in order, the x402 flow,

113
00:06:28,295 --> 00:06:30,326
and exactly who can sign what.

114
00:06:31,626 --> 00:06:33,025
Every rule is tested.

115
00:06:33,075 --> 00:06:37,440
30 LiteSVM tests run against the compiled program in Docker,

116
00:06:37,490 --> 00:06:41,441
covering caps, reputation, freezing, expiry and approvals.

117
00:06:41,491 --> 00:06:44,269
The program is deployed on Solana devnet,

118
00:06:44,319 --> 00:06:46,408
and the site deploys on Vercel.

119
00:06:47,708 --> 00:06:52,264
That's Allowance. Wallets for AI agents, with rules the chain enforces.

120
00:06:52,314 --> 00:06:56,869
Even if the AI or a server were compromised, the program still says no.

121
00:06:56,919 --> 00:06:59,010
It's live on Solana devnet today.
